1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,550
This video shows you how to turn raw text into typed fields.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,353
A firewall sends its log lines to Kafka.

3
00:00:07,353 --> 00:00:11,710
A source called firewall already reads them, on this branch.

4
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:15,833
Add a route from the firewall source, with a new pipeline.

5
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,624
Capture what's in the topic, from the earliest record.

6
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:30,022
Ten lines.

7
00:00:30,022 --> 00:00:32,341
Each one is plain text, not JSON.

8
00:00:32,341 --> 00:00:34,486
Save them as a sample.

9
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:45,510
smeltr recognizes the syslog header, and proposes a Parser step that reads it.

10
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:48,148
It reads the timestamp and the host.

11
00:00:48,148 --> 00:00:51,150
The rest of each line lands in message.

12
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,336
timestamp is still a string.

13
00:00:53,336 --> 00:00:57,978
Make it a datetime, so later steps can compare times.

14
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:01,502
message holds the firewall's own fields.

15
00:01:01,502 --> 00:01:04,606
Add a second Parser step to read it.

16
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:13,535
Under Advanced, set its input field to message, and choose Regex/Grok.

17
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:17,609
A Grok pattern names each part of the line.

18
00:01:17,609 --> 00:01:20,077
Each name becomes a field.

19
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:27,802
The fields appear as you type, each one a string.

20
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:31,058
Ports and bytes are numbers.

21
00:01:31,058 --> 00:01:33,163
Make them integers.

22
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:37,492
One line failed.

23
00:01:37,492 --> 00:01:41,772
It's the status line, which has no connection in it.

24
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:49,790
The record carries an Unparsable exception, and keeps the fields the earlier step read.

25
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:51,256
Add a second pattern.

26
00:01:51,256 --> 00:01:55,026
The first pattern that matches parses the line.

27
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:01,498
Now the status line parses too, into a field of its own.

28
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:05,991
Save the pipeline.

29
00:02:05,991 --> 00:02:12,521
The route already sends to firewall-parsed, the only destination, so save the route.

30
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:17,704
Check on learning.

31
00:02:17,904 --> 00:02:21,306
A line matches none of a Parser step's patterns.

32
00:02:21,306 --> 00:02:23,253
What happens to it?

33
00:02:27,653 --> 00:02:33,443
It carries an Unparsable exception, and keeps the fields an earlier step read.

34
00:02:34,343 --> 00:02:37,650
Why make ports and bytes integers?

35
00:02:42,050 --> 00:02:46,357
So later steps can calculate and compare with them as numbers.

36
00:02:47,257 --> 00:02:51,583
Next, you change these records with steps, and with code.
